==English==
{{wp}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|bench mark}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|bench|mark}}. First use appears c. 1842. Originally a mark cut into a stone by land surveyors to secure a ''bench'' (from land surveying jargon in the 19th century, meaning a type of [[bracket]]), to mount measuring equipment. The figurative sense first appears c. 1884.<ref>[http://www.etymologie.info/~e/u_/us-manage.html#Benchmark Benchmark etymology on etymologie.info]</ref>

